WEBVTT

00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:14.560
Chief! At noon, on the roof,

00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:17.563
and the sky was so blue!

00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:20.503
But I went back at dusk,

00:00:20.567 --> 00:00:23.777
and the sun was a big red ball!

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:27.380
Same sun, and the same sunlight.

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:32.450
You see it every day. So
who changed its color?

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.930
Someone swapped colors!

00:00:36.167 --> 00:00:41.127
Something's up to tricks. It's
right by you, but you can't see it.

00:00:42.367 --> 00:00:45.433
So the sky's blue,

00:00:45.433 --> 00:00:46.967
where's it come from?

00:00:46.967 --> 00:00:52.900
First, a guess: water, a few
drops of milk, a flashlight,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.430
from the side, what color is it?

00:00:56.267 --> 00:00:58.600
Water with milk?

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.110
Kind of white?

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.690
And on the Moon, by day, look up:

00:01:05.367 --> 00:01:07.747
what color is the sky?

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.900
The Moon? No idea.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:12.967
And there are clouds!

00:01:12.967 --> 00:01:17.477
If the sky's blue, why are clouds white?

00:01:18.167 --> 00:01:19.767
Same sun,

00:01:19.767 --> 00:01:23.287
so why blue, then red?

00:01:23.467 --> 00:01:26.027
Isn't the sky just blue?

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.830
If it were just blue,

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.270
why does it go black after sunset?

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:36.870
Because... the sun went home?

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.190
Yes. Sun gone, blue gone.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:43.057
So that blue comes from sunlight.

00:01:43.667 --> 00:01:46.107
But sunlight is white,

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.830
Where's blue?

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:51.330
White light is many colors mixed.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:54.650
They hit your eyes
together; you see white.

00:01:56.333 --> 00:02:00.613
Watering plants, you may see
a tiny rainbow in the spray.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:05.310
Those colors were hiding in sunlight.

00:02:05.933 --> 00:02:08.393
Point a glass prism at the sun,

00:02:09.867 --> 00:02:12.437
and a row of colors shows on the wall.

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:18.373
Long ago, the English scientist
Newton split sunlight with a prism.

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:25.537
Red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet!

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:28.867
All squeezed into white light?

00:02:28.867 --> 00:02:30.833
Yes, every one.

00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:32.783
White light is crowded!

00:02:34.167 --> 00:02:35.933
So colors,

00:02:35.933 --> 00:02:37.693
how are they different?

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:41.600
Ever swung a jump rope? It makes waves.

00:02:42.233 --> 00:02:45.443
Light travels like that, wave after wave.

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:50.833
Red has long waves, like big slow swings;

00:02:50.833 --> 00:02:54.193
blue and violet have short
ones, like quick shakes.

00:02:55.767 --> 00:02:58.967
Wave top to wave top: that's wavelength.

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.582
Different wavelengths, different colors.

00:03:04.233 --> 00:03:06.493
Faster, shorter waves!

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:10.123
So blue is a shorter wave?

00:03:10.767 --> 00:03:13.277
Yes. Remember that. It'll be back.

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.967
So is the sky blue

00:03:16.967 --> 00:03:19.277
because air is blue?

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:22.840
Take an empty jar, full of air.

00:03:23.933 --> 00:03:25.173
See any blue?

00:03:25.933 --> 00:03:27.433
I can't.

00:03:27.533 --> 00:03:29.013
It's clear.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:31.833
A jar of air: no color.

00:03:31.833 --> 00:03:35.953
But the air above your head
is a very, very thick layer.

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:43.233
From high up it's clear: US
Air Force photos at 20 km

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:46.753
and Earth's edge is a thin blue ring.

00:03:46.867 --> 00:03:50.507
That air is made of tiny, tiny molecules.

00:03:50.967 --> 00:03:53.897
Molecules? What are those?

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:56.850
Think of tiny balls in the air,

00:03:57.333 --> 00:03:58.673
too small to see.

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:03.317
I breathe in little balls too?

00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:07.977
Yes. Every breath, you
take in a huge number.

00:04:08.867 --> 00:04:13.267
A molecule is about 1,000 times
smaller than a light wave.

00:04:14.633 --> 00:04:16.800
1,000?

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.820
Then light just goes through?

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.020
Mostly, yes.

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:23.980
But some light hits them and bounces off,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:27.560
all ways: scattering.

00:04:27.833 --> 00:04:31.253
The bounced light hits other
balls and bounces again.

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.467
Like marbles!

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:40.707
A bit, but it bounces in every direction.

00:04:41.733 --> 00:04:43.133
Each one

00:04:43.133 --> 00:04:44.893
gets bounced?

00:04:45.433 --> 00:04:49.223
Yes. But remember those
short little waves?

00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:51.903
They get bounced the most.

00:04:52.833 --> 00:04:55.943
Blue gets bounced several
times more than red.

00:04:57.333 --> 00:05:00.803
Several times? Busy blue!

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.880
And red?

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:05.580
Red bounces aside less.

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:08.060
It mostly goes straight ahead.

00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:11.367
Red is so focused!

00:05:12.333 --> 00:05:15.653
In the 1800s, Englishman
Rayleigh did the math.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:20.297
So this is called Rayleigh scattering.

00:05:21.333 --> 00:05:24.093
Funny: back then, they thought

00:05:24.567 --> 00:05:27.767
dust and tiny water
drops bounced the blue.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.300
Later, scientists saw: with dust,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:35.720
the blue would vary by weather,

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:38.480
but it really doesn't.

00:05:39.133 --> 00:05:43.393
Then, in 1910, physicist
Einstein calculated

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.670
air molecules alone turn the sky blue.

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:49.133
Einstein too

00:05:49.133 --> 00:05:51.043
did sky math!

00:05:51.433 --> 00:05:53.813
So anywhere you look up,

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:56.880
bounced blue light is reaching your eyes.

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:00.033
Not blue;

00:06:00.033 --> 00:06:02.593
It keeps bouncing blue light to you.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:06.367
So the color thief

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:07.657
was the air!

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.103
But I heard

00:06:10.267 --> 00:06:12.737
the sky reflects the sea.

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.680
Many do.

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:20.723
But on a mountain far, far from
the sea, the sky's still blue.

00:06:21.233 --> 00:06:23.973
Often it's reversed: on a beach,

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.340
the sea looks blue
partly by mirroring sky.

00:06:28.933 --> 00:06:32.200
Wait, violet waves are shorter,

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:34.970
so why isn't the sky violet?

00:06:35.033 --> 00:06:38.993
Good one. Sunlight has
less violet to start.

00:06:39.567 --> 00:06:42.967
Some gets absorbed by air very high up.

00:06:43.367 --> 00:06:46.407
Swallowed: it never reaches the ground.

00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:50.187
The air sneaks a snack!

00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:53.567
And our eyes are less sensitive to violet.

00:06:54.433 --> 00:06:57.873
There's violet up there, mixed in,

00:06:58.033 --> 00:07:00.883
so to us it looks pale blue.

00:07:01.467 --> 00:07:02.900
My eye

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.933
pick colors too?

00:07:04.933 --> 00:07:06.653
That's the fun part:

00:07:07.367 --> 00:07:09.907
your eyes help pick the color.

00:07:10.667 --> 00:07:12.957
My eyes are a palette!

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:15.967
Lots of blue bounces off,

00:07:15.967 --> 00:07:18.957
so what color is the sun itself?

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:20.700
I

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.190
use yellow.

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:27.470
Lots of people do. But to see
the sun itself, leave the ground.

00:07:28.967 --> 00:07:31.767
From space, it's white.

00:07:31.767 --> 00:07:35.017
Up there, you're above most of the air.

00:07:36.367 --> 00:07:38.377
Here it looks yellow

00:07:38.833 --> 00:07:41.883
because some blue got
bounced away on the way.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:45.233
With less blue,

00:07:45.233 --> 00:07:47.753
why's the sun so bright?

00:07:48.033 --> 00:07:49.703
Not much is lost.

00:07:51.233 --> 00:07:55.063
On clear days, over half the
sunlight still reaches us.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.533
No wonder

00:07:58.533 --> 00:08:00.463
days are still bright!

00:08:00.667 --> 00:08:04.200
So at sunset, is the sun

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:06.067
burning hotter?

00:08:06.067 --> 00:08:09.467
The sun's the same at
noon and dusk. Not it.

00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:13.985
What changes is the light's path to you.

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:16.967
The path changes?

00:08:16.967 --> 00:08:19.237
The sun isn't farther.

00:08:19.700 --> 00:08:21.170
Same distance

00:08:21.433 --> 00:08:25.553
What changes is the air the
light must cross: much thicker.

00:08:28.467 --> 00:08:30.733
Sun overhead:

00:08:30.733 --> 00:08:33.743
light comes straight down: less air.

00:08:34.567 --> 00:08:37.600
Near sunset, light slants in,

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:39.500
skimming the ground.

00:08:40.333 --> 00:08:44.353
Sun on the horizon, the air light crosses

00:08:44.667 --> 00:08:47.587
is about 30-some times the overhead.

00:08:48.533 --> 00:08:50.400
30-some?

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:51.470
Thick!

00:08:51.567 --> 00:08:53.827
Light's long trip!

00:08:54.633 --> 00:08:59.163
On that long trip, blue
bounces off, off, and off.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:04.670
By the time it reaches you,
it's mostly red, orange.

00:09:04.833 --> 00:09:07.983
So the lower the sun, usually the redder.

00:09:08.900 --> 00:09:12.880
But even a red sun: don't stare
at it. It can hurt your eyes.

00:09:14.667 --> 00:09:16.633
And sunrise,

00:09:16.633 --> 00:09:18.003
Same thing?

00:09:18.467 --> 00:09:23.600
Yes. At sunrise light
also skims in at a slant,

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:25.120
just from the east.

00:09:26.333 --> 00:09:28.843
The blue bounced away,

00:09:29.167 --> 00:09:31.207
where does it go?

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.550
Into other skies.

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:36.490
While you're watching a red sun,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:41.860
blue from that light

00:09:42.033 --> 00:09:44.853
gives far places an afternoon blue sky.

00:09:46.067 --> 00:09:48.817
US weather scientist Corfidi said:

00:09:49.833 --> 00:09:53.433
sunsets are red because
day skies are blue.

00:09:54.233 --> 00:09:56.400
The sunset I see

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.720
is someone's blue sky!

00:09:58.867 --> 00:10:01.357
Now for the water with milk.

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.740
Light from the side; look from there:

00:10:06.833 --> 00:10:09.923
the water has a faint blue tint.

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:14.560
Facing the light,

00:10:14.867 --> 00:10:18.397
the light coming through
looks orange and red.

00:10:20.033 --> 00:10:21.467
A cup:

00:10:21.467 --> 00:10:23.533
sky and sunset!

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:25.433
What's the milk doing?

00:10:26.233 --> 00:10:29.663
Milk's tiny bits bounce light, like air.

00:10:30.200 --> 00:10:33.700
The farther light goes,
the more blue is lost.

00:10:34.767 --> 00:10:39.400
Try it: water, a few drops
of milk, a flashlight.

00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:42.040
No color? Add two more drops.

00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:44.270
I'll borrow Dad's!

00:10:45.333 --> 00:10:47.533
Then look out at the clouds.

00:10:47.533 --> 00:10:50.733
Same sun on clouds,

00:10:50.733 --> 00:10:54.267
so why aren't they blue or red?

00:10:54.267 --> 00:10:56.737
Clouds are water drops,

00:10:56.833 --> 00:11:00.873
and a water drop is much
bigger than an air molecule.

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:04.860
Big drops bounce all colors alike.

00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:08.690
All the colors bouncing
together mix into white.

00:11:11.733 --> 00:11:13.500
Fog too

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.380
are white too!

00:11:15.667 --> 00:11:18.087
Yes, fog is drops too.

00:11:18.767 --> 00:11:21.867
When the sun hits morning
fog, it's all white.

00:11:23.467 --> 00:11:25.667
And before rain,

00:11:25.667 --> 00:11:28.007
why do clouds turn gray?

00:11:28.533 --> 00:11:30.913
Thick clouds block much light,

00:11:31.733 --> 00:11:35.583
so less light below: they look gray, dark.

00:11:35.867 --> 00:11:38.647
Sometimes it's the bright sky beside it

00:11:39.767 --> 00:11:42.297
next to it, your eyes see gray.

00:11:43.133 --> 00:11:45.673
Eyes picking colors again!

00:11:46.267 --> 00:11:48.897
But clouds at sunset

00:11:49.000 --> 00:11:51.800
are orange and pink!

00:11:52.933 --> 00:11:55.173
Their light was already bounced red.

00:11:56.533 --> 00:12:00.033
A cloud's a big screen,
bouncing color to you.

00:12:01.367 --> 00:12:03.567
The sky's a movie!

00:12:04.067 --> 00:12:07.497
Water drops change sky
colors, and so does dust.

00:12:08.967 --> 00:12:11.267
So more dust

00:12:11.267 --> 00:12:13.317
means bluer sky?

00:12:13.367 --> 00:12:15.367
Remember the old guess?

00:12:15.933 --> 00:12:19.713
You think like them, but
the answer is the opposite.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.267
Dust and smoke,

00:12:23.267 --> 00:12:27.497
so the day's blue fades,
like a white veil.

00:12:27.767 --> 00:12:29.467
That's called haze.

00:12:30.567 --> 00:12:33.957
With lots of haze, even
noon sky looks white.

00:12:34.867 --> 00:12:36.927
So the bluest days

00:12:37.333 --> 00:12:38.833
often have clean air.

00:12:40.133 --> 00:12:44.423
No wonder some days the sky is extra blue!

00:12:44.633 --> 00:12:46.767
Lots of dust,

00:12:46.767 --> 00:12:48.717
what about sunsets?

00:12:49.400 --> 00:12:52.020
Hard to say: sometimes redder,

00:12:52.767 --> 00:12:55.357
sometimes just gray and hazy.

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:00.950
Fly on a hazy evening: the
sunset below looks pale;

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:06.380
once the plane climbs
above the haze, colors pop.

00:13:07.100 --> 00:13:09.440
The haze hid the colors!

00:13:10.367 --> 00:13:14.057
Haze stays low, so high
clouds glow reddest:

00:13:14.900 --> 00:13:17.190
their light skipped the haze.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.250
High clouds glow reddest!

00:13:21.167 --> 00:13:25.007
An even stranger glow came from a volcano.

00:13:25.433 --> 00:13:30.223
In 1991, Mount Pinatubo in
the Philippines erupted.

00:13:30.467 --> 00:13:34.777
Its gas turned into fine
droplets high in the sky,

00:13:35.133 --> 00:13:38.743
spreading out about 20 to 30 km up.

00:13:39.833 --> 00:13:41.600
So high?

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:43.650
Seen from the ground?

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:46.460
15 minutes after sunset,

00:13:46.833 --> 00:13:52.123
but that high layer still caught
sunlight, glowing lilac and orange-red.

00:13:54.833 --> 00:13:58.153
These glows lingered about 18 months.

00:13:58.967 --> 00:14:02.557
Even in the US, across the
world, people saw them.

00:14:03.700 --> 00:14:05.380
A volcano

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:07.750
change color!

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:10.420
See a strange sunset? Think:

00:14:11.433 --> 00:14:14.173
what's extra in the air today?

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:19.200
More air changes colors.
What about almost none?

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.430
Remember the first question?
On the Moon, by day, look up.

00:14:24.967 --> 00:14:26.833
On the Moon...

00:14:26.833 --> 00:14:28.703
is there air?

00:14:28.767 --> 00:14:32.777
Almost none. Only a very, very thin trace.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.667
No air, no little balls

00:14:36.667 --> 00:14:38.733
to bounce blue to me...

00:14:38.733 --> 00:14:41.200
So the sky is black!

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:46.170
Yes. The sun is bright, yet the
Moon's daytime sky is black.

00:14:46.800 --> 00:14:49.720
Bright ground under a pitch-black sky.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:52.300
Oh!

00:14:52.300 --> 00:14:54.950
Remember my blue and red from the roof?

00:14:56.567 --> 00:14:58.167
Same light,

00:14:58.167 --> 00:15:00.757
short path and long path!

00:15:01.100 --> 00:15:03.670
Next time you look up, think:

00:15:04.100 --> 00:15:07.800
how far did this light
travel to reach you?

00:15:08.667 --> 00:15:10.577
So that's why!

00:15:11.233 --> 00:15:14.233
Your best sunset color ever?

00:15:14.233 --> 00:15:16.373
Where? Tell us in comments.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:22.010
Want to find life's whys with
us? Subscribe to So That's Why.
